      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-07T17:18:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This should be unsafe as it may leave the MMIO in a partially unmapped state. For example, client code could assume that it is fine to log an error on failure while the UART page has already been unmapped.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 240,
        "startChar": 44,
        "endLine": 240,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "79a0658bb7efd2b9ee81b6218432e20c260436fa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "750ffe8c_222d872a",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/memory.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 240,
      "author": {
        "id": 1380243
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T14:05:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If so, then `mmio_guard::unmap` should also be marked as `unsafe`. But I\u0027m not convinced that it should. Functions need to be marked as `unsafe` if they can cause undefined behaviour, but the definition of undefined behaviour at https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/what-unsafe-does.html doesn\u0027t include aborting the program, which is what will happen if we try to access MMIO space unmapped from the MMIO guard, right? In fact it specifically says that Rust considers it safe to abort the program.",
      "parentUuid": "eb59b561_9c373fa9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 240,
        "startChar": 44,
        "endLine": 240,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "79a0658bb7efd2b9ee81b6218432e20c260436fa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "963aef8f_dfce17ac",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/memory.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 240,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T14:19:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This feels like splitting hair but I agree with you that both functions should have the same qualifier.\n\nThanks for the link, it\u0027s an interesting read (although I remain convinced that `unsafe` was defined in the context of userspace code (including `std` implementation) and not baremetal code that directly controls and sets up the abstractions provided by the hardware).\n\nI suppose the excerpt relevant to my point above would have been:\n\n\u003e Of course, unsafe functions and traits are free to declare arbitrary other constraints that a program must maintain to avoid Undefined Behavior.\n\nAs the behavior is defined by the exception handler, as you pointed out.\n\n(marking as resolved)",
      "parentUuid": "750ffe8c_222d872a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 240,
        "startChar": 44,
        "endLine": 240,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "79a0658bb7efd2b9ee81b6218432e20c260436fa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
